    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-6-981-2013",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Quantifying the impact of rotational-Raman scattering (RRS) on the O2 A- and B-bands is important as these bands can be used for cloud and aerosol characterization for trace-gas retrievals including CO2 and CH4. In this paper, we simulate the spectral effects of RRS for various viewing geometries and instruments with different spectral resolutions. We also examine how aerosols affect the amount of RRS filling-in. We show that the filling-in effects of RRS are relatively small, but not negligible, in these O2 absorption bands, particularly for high-spectral-resolution instruments. For comparison, we also compare and contrast the spectral signatures of RRS with those of terrestrial chlorophyll fluorescence.",
    "field": "Atmospheric remote sensing",
    "problem": "The paper addresses how rotational-Raman scattering, a previously neglected atmospheric effect, can fill in O\u2082 absorption bands, creating an additive signal that may bias high-precision satellite retrievals of trace gases like CO\u2082 and CH\u2084, especially as accuracy requirements for these measurements increase.",
    "method": "This paper uses radiative transfer simulations to quantify the spectral filling-in effects of rotational-Raman scattering in the O2 A- and B-bands under various viewing geometries and atmospheric conditions, comparing these effects with those of terrestrial chlorophyll fluorescence to assess their impact on remote sensing retrievals.",
    "task": "The task is to simulate and quantify the spectral effects of rotational-Raman scattering (RRS) in the O2 A- and B-bands to assess its impact on satellite retrievals of trace-gases and compare its signature to that of terrestrial chlorophyll fluorescence.",
